  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Facilitate and institutionalize dialogue on socio economic issues including policy formulation and implementation and  capacity building of the Tripartite Negotiation Forum (TNF)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">During the 2022 annual planning meeting, the social partners raised concerns on statutory instruments that where being passed by the government during the COVID-19 period without consultations. Concerns were also raised on other outstanding operational modalities for the TNF including the SOP and outstanding bills that are yet to be discussed by the TNF. These suggest for a need to capacity build the TNF. Looking at the economy, even before the outbreak and spread of the COVID-19 pandemic, Zimbabwe was already in crisis, real GDP having declined by -6.5% in 2019. Since 2021, the economy has been on a positive growth trajectory with real GDP growth of 8.5% in 2021, 6.5% in 2022, a projected growth rate of 5.5% in 2023, projected 3.5% in 2024 and 5% in 2025. Zimbabwe&#x2019;s experience with social dialogue: Section 13(2) of the Constitution mandates Government to &#x201C;&#x2026;involve the people in the formulation and implementation of development plans and programmes that affect them.&#x201D; This is reinforced in the TNF Act, which was promulgated in June 2019.</narrative>
  </description>
